Jess (Sales): FYI Aurelia Health POC is seeing JSON parse errors in their web client when streaming via SSE. They say it works for short responses but breaks mid-stream. :frowning_face:
Carla (Client): repro steps: open SSE, start request, after ~3 chunks the browser console shows `SyntaxError: Unexpected token \u0000 in JSON at position 0` and the stream stops. Not seeing server 5xx.
Ben (SE): can you paste the raw SSE payload around the failure? need to see delimiters and whether event/data lines are split across chunks.
Carla (Client): here's a snippet from Chrome Network > Response (copied):
```
event: completion
data: {"id":"abc123","delta":"first chunk"}


event: completion
data: {"id":"abc123","delta":"second chunk"}


event: completion
data: {"id":"abc123","delta":"third chunk"}
\u0000

```
Carla (Client): note the trailing NUL (\u0000) bytes after the third chunk. we didn't emit that.
Ben (SE): that NUL is suspicious — commonly introduced by a proxy or when the server writes a padded buffer. Are you running behind nginx/Cloudflare or an app proxy?
Carla (Client): yes nginx reverse proxy in front of our app. We also have Cloudflare spectrum on the domain for now.
Diego (Oncall): tagging infra — nginx by default can buffer and re-chunk responses. If a backend writes fixed-size buffers, leftover bytes might be padded. Try disabling proxy_buffering and gzip for the SSE route.
Evan (Eng): on Redwood side, we've seen chunk boundary issues when upstream sets `Content-Length` or some middle proxy does reassembly. Our runtime expects proper SSE framing (blank line between events). If extra bytes are injected, client JSON.parse will choke.
Ben (SE): quick diagnosis steps for Carla: 1) curl with `-N` to stream raw bytes from upstream (bypass browser). run `curl -N -v https://your-poc-url/stream` and save to file. 2) hexdump the bytes: `xxd -g 1 stream.out | sed -n '1,120p'` 3) check for 00 bytes or stray CRLF sequences.
Carla (Client): ran curl -N, pipe to xxd, got this chunk: `... 7d 0a 0a 65 76 65 6e 74 3a 20 63 6f 6d 70 6c 65 74 69 6f 6e 0a 64 61 74 61 3a 20 7b 22 69 64 22 3a 22 61 62 63 31 32 33 22 2c 22 64 65 6c 74 61 22 3a 22 74 68 69 72 64 20 63 68 75 6e 6b 22 7d 00 0a 0a`
Ben (SE): there it is: `00` byte after the JSON object before the blank line. That will cause a `JSON.parse` failure if the client concatenates and tries to parse including that byte.
Diego (Oncall): possible causes: (a) nginx proxy buffering with `proxy_buffering on;` causing partial buffer paddings, (b) backend writing fixed-size chunked buffers with zero padding, (c) TCP keepalive/stream decoders introducing sentinel bytes.
ops-bot: CI deploy to staging finished for `poc/sse-fix` (build #412). :rocket:
Ben (SE): short-term workaround for the client: treat SSE data as text, sanitize before JSON.parse: `const s = data.replace(/\u0000/g,''); JSON.parse(s)` — not ideal but buys time. Longer term, fix the source of the padding.
Evan (Eng): on Redwood runtime, we can toggle `sse-strict=true` for this tenant to ensure we flush exact event boundaries without padding. Emily, do we need customer consent to flip tenant runtime flags?
Emily (Presales): flipping a non-invasive streaming flag is fine for the POC; just document it in the POC notes and update the support ticket.
Carla (Client): any nginx config examples?
Diego (Oncall): try adding to your location block for the stream endpoint: `proxy_buffering off; proxy_request_buffering off; gzip off; chunked_transfer_encoding on; proxy_set_header Connection ''; proxy_http_version 1.1;` and also `proxy_read_timeout 3600;`
Ben (SE): also check `proxy_set_header Transfer-Encoding '';` — sometimes misconfigured headers cause proxies to add padding. If you're using Cloudflare, enable 'Disable HTTP/2 buffering' or put the domain on a Page Rule to bypass features for this route.
Carla (Client): changed nginx to `proxy_buffering off` and redeployed. Re-ran curl and xxd — the \u0000 bytes are gone. Browser client still seeing occasional partial JSON error but much less frequently.
Evan (Eng): could be a timing edge where the server writes a partial event across two writes and the client attempts to parse before the blank line arrives. Client should accumulate until it sees `\n\n` separator. Ben, can you paste a simple client pattern?
Ben (SE): Node example to accumulate SSE events:```
let buffer = '';
res.on('data', chunk => {
  buffer += chunk.toString('utf8');
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buffer.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1) {
    const evt = buffer.slice(0, idx).trim();
    buffer = buffer.slice(idx + 2);
    // extract lines starting with 'data: '
    const dataLines = evt.split('\n').filter(l => l.startsWith('data:'));
    const payload = dataLines.map(l => l.replace(/^data:\s*/,'')).join('\n');
    try { const parsed = JSON.parse(payload); /* handle */ } catch(e) { console.warn('parse fail', e, payload); }
  }
});
```
Carla (Client): implemented buffer logic; seems stable now. thanks.
Jess (Sales): good — client happy for now. two action items: 1) Evan/Diego to confirm nginx + CF recommended config for docs, 2) Ben/Emily to add a short POC note with the workaround and runtime flag we toggled.
Diego (Oncall): I'll post an ops note in the infra playbook and add a snippet for `proxy_buffering off` and `proxy_request_buffering off`. Also will scan our edge fleet for any similar padding regressions.
Evan (Eng): i'll open internal ticket to make `sse-strict` safer and default it for new POCs (link in thread once created).
Ben (SE): FYI also suggested to Aurelia: if you see `NUL` bytes in other routes, check your app server write logic (zeroing mmapped buffers, using `sendfile` with preallocated files).
Carla (Client): thanks all — sending this in the POC wrap-up doc.
Jess (Sales): awesome, Emily can you add the config snippet to the SOW / next-step checklist?
Emily (Presales): will do, added to POC checklist and security notes.
Jess (Sales): closing the loop — great team swoop. :tada:
